; freqmeter.pe -- an input frequency and duty meter: the pin is an INPUT and
; this program recovers two numbers from a waveform it does not control.
;
; THE ACT. Six of the timing acts in this repository DRIVE a pad. This one
; LISTENS, which is a different shape of problem: the PWM arrives from
; outside, the program cannot ask when the edges are coming, and the answer it
; has to produce is a count between two edges it did not schedule. The number
; that is the claim is the ACCURACY OF THE COUNT.
;
; WHAT IS MEASURED, per run: two 16-bit pairs -- (period, high time), in whole
; microseconds -- for two consecutive periods of the incoming PWM. The
; testbench sweeps the input over 158 Hz to 10 kHz with a varying duty and
; checks every banked point, not the ends of the sweep.
;
; WHY THE LOW END IS THE POINT OF THE ACT. At 100 Hz the period is 10 000 us,
; which does not fit in one 8-bit counter. A firmware that counts into a byte
; wraps and reports 16 us for a 100 Hz signal: a wrong ANSWER rather than a
; wrong precision, and a defect with no symptom at 10 kHz. That is why the
; counters below are 16-bit, and why the mutation gate perturbs the high byte
; specifically.
;
; THE TIMEBASE, and the trap in it. This SoC has TWO tick counters and they
; are not the same size. Port 7 (STATUS) is the UART HALF-BIT tick: 260
; clocks, 4.33 us, because it exists to place a serial sample mid-cell. Port
; 4 (I2CTICK) is the free-running 1 us counter, and port 6 is its clear-on-
; read FLAG. The first version of this program counted STATUS, and measured
; 0.24 ticks per microsecond -- a factor of 4.33 out, reporting a 200 us
; period as 46. A tick counter whose name says TIMER is not a microsecond.
;
; And the flag is not used either. A clear-on-read flag is one bit, so a poll
; loop that takes longer than a tick reports several ticks as one, and the
; loss is invisible in the result. This program instead READS THE COUNTER
; and adds the DIFFERENCE:
;
;     elapsed = (now - previous) mod 256
;
; which is exact for any loop up to 255 us long, and this loop is 1.3 us --
; two hundred times inside it. There is no flag to lose, so a slow iteration
; (and the bank below is the slowest thing in the program) makes the
; measurement LATE BY EXACTLY THE RIGHT AMOUNT rather than wrong. The
; difference is also why the subtraction needs no carry test: the borrow of
; (previous - now) is only ever used as "is it zero", which is the one
; comparison the ISA has.
;
; THE ISA CONSTRAINT THAT SHAPES THE REST. The machine is 8-bit with no carry
; flag -- only JZ/JNZ exist -- so 16-bit arithmetic is hand-built. A 16-bit
; INCREMENT is the cheap case. A 16-bit ADD of a VARIABLE is the one this
; program needs, and it is affordable because of something specific to it: the
; addend is the elapsed time, which is never zero, so "the low byte came out
; zero" means the low byte WRAPPED and the high byte must be incremented,
; with no both-zero case to guard against. A program adding two memory values
; would need that guard, because 0 + 0 = 0 is not a carry.
;
; A 16-bit SUBTRACT is a different matter entirely and this program contains
; none. That is the design:
;
;     THE PERIOD IS T AT THE RISING EDGE, BECAUSE T IS RESET AT EVERY RISING
;     EDGE. THE HIGH TIME IS H AT THE RISING EDGE, BECAUSE H IS ALSO RESET
;     THERE AND STOPS COUNTING AT THE FALLING EDGE.
;
; Two resets remove every subtract, every compare and every divide. A 16-bit
; borrow test is a four-case analysis on bit 7 and a compare is the same
; thing again; neither appears here because neither is needed. The two
; counters are the elapsed time SINCE THE LAST EDGE rather than a difference
; of two absolute times, and that is also why the measurement is immune to a
; tick the program failed to notice.
;
; THE RESOLUTION IS ONE MICROSECOND, and that is the honest specification of
; this instrument: the counter is in microseconds, so the relative error is
; 1/period -- 0.01 % at 100 Hz and 1.0 % at 10 kHz. The testbench's tolerance
; is therefore 1 % of the period with a floor of one and a half ticks, stated
; per point, because the instrument's resolution IS the claim. Counting CLOCKS
; would be finer, and the way to get that is to count the pin rather than the
; tick; the 1 us counter is used here because it is the timebase this SoC
; has, and the pin is sampled every 14 clocks (0.23 us) so the EDGE is found
; well inside the microsecond the counter then rounds it to.
;
; MEMORY. This machine has SIXTEEN bytes of data memory (DMEM_BYTES = 16 in
; rtl/pe_soc.v, and every top level passes 16). The map below uses all
; sixteen, which is why the program banks two points per run rather than a
; whole sweep:
;
;     0,1   dmem[0..1]  T, the period counter, reset at every rising edge
;     2,3   dmem[2..3]  H, the high-time counter, reset at every rising edge
;     4     dmem[4]     the previous pad level (00 or 40)
;     5     dmem[5]     the slot the next pair is banked into: FF before the
;                       first rising edge, then 0, then 1, then 2 when both
;                       are banked
;     6     dmem[6]     1 when the run has banked both slots
;     7     dmem[7]     the previous reading of the 1 us counter
;     8,9   slot 0 period      10,11  slot 0 high time
;    12,13  slot 1 period      14,15  slot 1 high time
;
; dmem[5] doubling as the "not started yet" state is how a separate FIRST
; byte was avoided: the program needs one more byte than the sixteen it has,
; and the slot index is the one value that is not a measurement.
;
; The first rising edge banks NOTHING, and cannot: the program started in the
; middle of a period, so the time since the previous rising edge is not a
; period. That is a property of the protocol rather than a bug -- a receiver
; that starts mid-period misses that one period -- and it is why the
; testbench presents each run's first period as a warm-up and does not check
; it. The slot base addresses are the constants FM_PER_BASE (8) and
; FM_HI_BASE (10) in tools/fw/peasm.py, and the mutation gate perturbs them:
; on a sixteen-byte machine a shifted base overlaps the working counters
; rather than running off the end of memory.
;
; REQUIREMENT ON THE SIGNAL, and it is a real one: the line idles HIGH. A PWM
; output idles high between frames, so the previous-level byte is initialised
; to the high level and the initial high is not mistaken for a rising edge. A
; line that idles low would need that byte initialised to 0; it is one
; immediate, and stating it is better than a program that silently assumes.

        ; ---- init ------------------------------------------------------
init:
        LDI   A, 0
        OUT   PINOE, A        ; release EVERY pad: this program only listens.
                               ; PINOE is a whole register -- a write meaning
                               ; "this pin" is also a write meaning "the other
                               ; pin", which has bitten this project four times.
        IN    A, I2CTICK      ; the 1 us counter, seeded from where it is NOW
        STM   7, A            ; rather than from zero, or the first elapsed
                               ; time would be the counter's whole age
        LDI   A, FM_IN
        STM   4, A             ; the line idles high (see the header)
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   0, A             ; T = 0
        STM   2, A             ; H = 0
        STM   6, A             ; not done
        LDI   A, 0xFF
        STM   5, A             ; no rising edge has been seen yet

        ; ---- the poll loop ----------------------------------------------
        ; The pin is sampled on EVERY iteration, not only when a microsecond
        ; has passed: the edge is then found to within one iteration (14
        ; clocks, 0.23 us) rather than to within a whole microsecond, and the
        ; counter still rounds the result to microseconds because that is
        ; what a microsecond counter counts.
main:
        IN    A, I2CTICK       ; the free-running 1 us counter
        LDM   X, 7             ; X = the previous reading
        MOV   Y, A             ; Y = now
        MOV   A, Y
        STM   7, A             ; the previous reading becomes NOW, whether or
                               ; not a microsecond has passed -- on the
                               ; iterations where none has, it is unchanged
        SUB   A, X             ; A = now - previous. One subtraction does
        JZ    the_pin          ; both jobs: ZERO means no microsecond has
                               ; passed, and NONZERO means A IS the elapsed
                               ; time, 1..255. The ISA has no carry flag and
                               ; this is the only way to get it: the borrow of
                               ; (now - previous) is thrown away and the
                               ; VALUE is what matters.
        MOV   X, A             ; X = the elapsed time

        ; --- T += elapsed -------------------------------------------
        LDM   A, 0
        ADD   A, X
        STM   0, A
        JZ    t_carry          ; the low byte wrapped, so the high one must
        JMP   t_done           ; too. Both zero is impossible: the addend is
t_carry:                       ; the elapsed time and is never zero.
        LDM   A, 1
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   1, A
t_done:
        ; --- H += elapsed, the high-time counter ---------------------
        LDM   A, 2
        ADD   A, X
        STM   2, A
        JZ    h_carry
        JMP   the_pin
h_carry:
        LDM   A, 3
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   3, A

        ; ---- the pad, sampled every iteration -------------------------
        ; Both values are 00 or FM_IN, so A - X is zero EXACTLY when the level
        ; is unchanged. This is the ISA's only byte comparison and the whole
        ; firmware rests on it: JZ/JNZ are the only flags there are.
the_pin:
        IN    A, PIN            ; driven pins read back, released pins the pad
        AND   A, FM_IN
        MOV   X, A              ; X = the level now
        LDM   A, 4
        SUB   A, X
        JZ    main              ; unchanged: no edge, go round again

        MOV   A, X
        JZ    fall              ; A == 0: the line went LOW
        JMP   rise

        ; ---- a FALLING edge: the high time is complete ----------------
fall:
        MOV   A, X
        STM   4, A             ; the level now, which is 0

        ; H is banked straight into the slot dmem[5] names. It is written HERE
        ; and not at the rising edge because H is live in between, and the
        ; falling edge is the only instant at which it holds the high time and
        ; nothing else. The stride between the slots is four bytes, so the
        ; address is FM_HI_BASE + 4*IDX, and 4*IDX is two doublings -- a
        ; 16-bit-free add of a VARIABLE, the one the ISA can express:
        ; ADD A, X.
        LDM   A, 5
        MOV   X, A
        ADD   A, X              ; 2 * IDX
        MOV   X, A
        ADD   A, X              ; 4 * IDX
        MOV   X, A
        LDI   A, FM_HI_BASE
        ADD   A, X
        MOV   X, A              ; X = the high-time address of this slot
        LDM   A, 2
        STS   [X], A
        INCX
        LDM   A, 3
        STS   [X], A
        JMP   main

        ; ---- a RISING edge: the period is complete, and H is the high --
rise:
        MOV   A, X
        STM   4, A             ; the level now, which is FM_IN

        LDM   A, 5
        MOV   X, A
        LDI   A, 0xFF
        SUB   A, X              ; zero exactly when no rising edge has been
        JZ    first_rise        ; seen yet, which is the reference edge

        LDM   A, 5
        MOV   X, A
        ADD   A, X
        MOV   X, A
        ADD   A, X
        MOV   X, A              ; X = 4 * IDX
        LDI   A, FM_PER_BASE
        ADD   A, X
        MOV   X, A              ; X = the period address of this slot
        LDM   A, 0
        STS   [X], A
        INCX
        LDM   A, 1
        STS   [X], A

        LDM   A, 5
        JNZ   fin               ; IDX was 1, so both slots are now full
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   5, A
        JMP   rearm

first_rise:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   5, A             ; slot 0 is next; nothing is banked yet
        JMP   rearm

fin:
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   5, A             ; both slots are banked, and the index says so
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   6, A             ; DONE
        JMP   park

        ; ---- rearm: both counters restart from this edge ---------------
        ; The reset comes AFTER the bank, on purpose. A reset before it would
        ; bank zero -- and a zero period is a value no check on the duty can
        ; explain, which is why the two were written in this order rather than
        ; the other way round.
rearm:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   0, A
        STM   1, A
        STM   2, A
        STM   3, A
        JMP   main

park:
        JMP   park              ; the run is finished; the host reads dmem
